  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nQuiet weather will continue across central Illinois this\nafternoon and evening as convection develops near a lingering\noutflow boundary along/north of the I-80 corridor where 19z/2pm\nMLCAPEs have exceeded 4000J/kg. Given the lack of a clear\nforcing mechanism and an overall more stable environment due to\nthe presence of a mid-level cloud deck, think CAMs such as the\nHRRR and RRFS are correct in keeping central Illinois dry and \ndeveloping storms across northern Illinois, then tracking them \ngenerally eastward into the Chicago metro and eventually into \nsouthern Michigan/northern Indiana this evening. Additional \nstorm clusters may form overnight as the low-level jet \nstrengthens, but these will largely stay north of the KILX CWA \nas well.\n\nConfidence is growing that a more significant cluster of\nthunderstorms will form over northern Iowa late tonight, then\nrace E/SE across northern Illinois/Indiana on Tuesday. The 12z\nHRRR/RRFS are in relatively good agreement concerning the\ntrack/timing of the storms...taking the most significant wind \nrisk along/just north of the northern KILX CWA boundary during \nthe morning into the early afternoon. The latest Day 2 Convective\nOutlook highlights locations north of I-74 with an Enhanced \nRisk (level 3 of 5) for severe weather. Wind gusts in excess of\n70mph will be the primary hazard: however, a few tornadoes may\nbe embedded within the leading edge of the squall line as well.\nModel consensus generally focuses the strongest wind gusts along\nI-80 into north-central Indiana: however, it appears likely that\nthe trailing end of the line will graze the I-74 corridor. Aside\nfrom the convection, Tuesday is also shaping up to be a very\nwarm and humid day. The big question will be how far south the\nstorms and/or the resulting outflow boundary will settle...as\nthis will significantly impact the temp/heat index forecast.\nBased on the 12z HRRR/RRFS, have opted to issue a Heat Advisory\nalong/south of a Macomb...to Lincoln...to Casey line where\nafternoon heat index values will peak at 100-105. Further N/NE,\noutflow from the early days storms will keep temps a bit cooler.\n\nOnce the initial severe storm cluster exits into Indiana, will\nneed to keep an eye on possible convective development along the\ntrailing outflow during the afternoon/evening. Many CAMs are\ninitially fairly sparse with convective coverage, but then\ndevelop activity after dark as the nocturnal jet cranks up. As\nsuch, have kept afternoon PoPs confined to 20-30% mostly east \nof I-55. Areal coverage will increase into the 30-50% range\nafter dark as the outflow boundary serves as the primary\nfocusing mechanism.\n\nW/NW upper flow will keep central Illinois in an active\nconvective pattern through the remainder of the week. As the\nprimary baroclinic zone wavers across the area, several rounds\nof showers and thunderstorms will occur Wednesday through\nSaturday. While it will not be a complete wash-out on any of\nthose days, the risk for occasional severe weather/heavy\nrainfall will be persistent. Given the favorable synoptic\npattern, the latest WPC 7-day QPF shows widespread amounts of \n1.50 to 2.50 along/north of a Quincy to Lawrenceville line. \nThere will likely be swaths of even heavier rainfall depending \non exactly where each convective complex ultimately tracks. \nStay tuned to later forecasts for additional details.",
